{"wiki_catalogs":[{"id":"3bd657af-017c-47c7-8642-5536567a1001","repo_id":"fc565b46-fc33-4061-a286-40b2001ec17d","name":"Project Overview","description":"project-overview","prompt":"Create comprehensive content for the Timeskein project overview section focused on the system's core purpose and philosophy. Explain the personal contextual journal concept, its metaphorical foundation, and how it transforms digital activity traces into structured episodes and meaning threads. Document the six core principles (local-first processing, data minimization, controlled sensitivity, provenance tracking, fault tolerance, portability), the target audience and use cases, and the high-level architecture overview. Include practical examples demonstrating how users can answer fundamental questions about their work activities. Provide conceptual overviews for beginners while establishing the technical foundation for experienced developers. Use terminology consistent with the codebase including terms like Work Items, Episodes, Threads, and Artifacts.","progress_status":"completed","dependent_files":"docs/00_project_overview.md","gmt_create":"2026-01-28T00:22:33.5803602+03:00","gmt_modified":"2026-01-28T00:23:52.0110591+03:00","raw_data":"WikiEncrypted:"},{"id":"9d6b3554-62ce-4a9e-ba78-ededed4de99e","repo_id":"fc565b46-fc33-4061-a286-40b2001ec17d","name":"Architecture Decision Records","description":"architecture-decision-records","prompt":"Create comprehensive content for the Architecture Decision Records section focused on Timeskein's foundational architectural choices. Document the initial architecture decisions, design rationale behind key technical choices, and trade-offs considered during system design. Explain the reasoning for local-first architecture, event sourcing patterns, and component separation strategies. Include the architectural patterns employed such as Ports \u0026 Adapters, Hexagonal Architecture, and Local-first principles. Document the decision-making process, constraints, and future evolution considerations. Provide technical details for experienced developers while explaining the implications for system behavior and extensibility. Address how these decisions impact the overall system design and future development direction.","order":1,"progress_status":"completed","dependent_files":"docs/adr/0001-initial-architecture.md","gmt_create":"2026-01-28T00:22:33.5814001+03:00","gmt_modified":"2026-01-28T00:23:58.311736+03:00","raw_data":"WikiEncrypted:"},{"id":"cfff1cae-2c0e-4438-9a17-6896416cd618","repo_id":"fc565b46-fc33-4061-a286-40b2001ec17d","name":"MVP Documentation","description":"mvp-documentation","prompt":"Create comprehensive content for the MVP documentation section focused on the minimum viable product specifications and user stories. Document the core user story for manual inventory management, detailed acceptance criteria, and user interaction scenarios. Explain the manual inventory requirements, UI/UX specifications, and user workflow patterns. Include detailed descriptions of user stories covering work item creation, state management, note-taking functionality, and reference attachment. Document the user interface design principles, interaction patterns, and accessibility considerations. Provide concrete examples from the actual user stories and explain how these requirements translate into system functionality. Address both functional requirements for beginners and implementation details for developers.","order":2,"progress_status":"completed","dependent_files":"docs/mvp/01_user_story_inventory.md,docs/mvp/02_user_story_manual_inventory.md,docs/mvp/02_manual_inventory_ui_ux.md","gmt_create":"2026-01-28T00:22:33.5814001+03:00","gmt_modified":"2026-01-28T00:23:51.1280774+03:00","raw_data":"WikiEncrypted:"},{"id":"bdf68ab6-3405-4f87-a795-6c7609a90a4c","repo_id":"fc565b46-fc33-4061-a286-40b2001ec17d","name":"Technical Design Specifications","description":"technical-design-specifications","prompt":"Create comprehensive content for the Technical Design Specifications section focused on Timeskein's component architecture and system design. Document the MVP inventory design, system topology, and client app suite architecture. Explain the component relationships, data flow patterns, and integration mechanisms between TS-AGENT, TS-DESKTOP, TS-ANDROID, TS-HUB, and TS-SCHEMA components. Include detailed descriptions of the ports \u0026 adapters pattern, hexagonal architecture implementation, and event-driven communication protocols. Document the API specifications for Local API, Event Ingest API, and sync protocols. Provide architectural diagrams and component breakdowns showing system boundaries and interaction patterns. Address infrastructure requirements, scalability considerations, and deployment topology. Include technical decisions, trade-offs, and constraints that shaped the current design.","order":3,"progress_status":"completed","dependent_files":"docs/rfc/0001-mvp-inventory-design.md,docs/rfc/0002-system-topology-and-component-map.md,docs/rfc/0003-client-app-suite-architecture.md","gmt_create":"2026-01-28T00:22:33.5819226+03:00","gmt_modified":"2026-01-28T00:25:16.8105531+03:00","raw_data":"WikiEncrypted:"},{"id":"4c6a73cb-175e-493e-b48c-4773e245bdd0","repo_id":"fc565b46-fc33-4061-a286-40b2001ec17d","name":"Development Roadmap","description":"development-roadmap","prompt":"Create comprehensive content for the Development Roadmap section focused on Timeskein's implementation timeline and future development plans. Document the MVP execution roadmap, milestone planning, and feature development sequence. Explain the phased approach to implementing core functionality, from manual inventory to advanced features like Episodes, Threads, semantic search, and multi-device synchronization. Include detailed timelines, deliverables, and success criteria for each phase. Document the transition from MVP to enhanced functionality, including connector development for applications like Obsidian, Slack, GitHub, and Jira. Address the optional server architecture, hub backend coordination, and conflict resolution strategies. Provide guidance for contributors and stakeholders on project progression and feature prioritization.","order":4,"progress_status":"completed","dependent_files":"docs/roadmap/0001-mvp-execution-roadmap.md","gmt_create":"2026-01-28T00:22:33.5819226+03:00","gmt_modified":"2026-01-28T00:24:54.6636671+03:00","raw_data":"WikiEncrypted:"}],"wiki_items":[{"catalog_id":"cfff1cae-2c0e-4438-9a17-6896416cd618","title":"MVP Documentation","description":"mvp-documentation","extend":"{}","progress_status":"completed","repo_id":"fc565b46-fc33-4061-a286-40b2001ec17d","id":"73072edc-b8fd-4cdc-bc12-0abf1ffe74e6","gmt_create":"2026-01-28T00:23:51.1232436+03:00","gmt_modified":"2026-01-28T00:23:51.1280774+03:00"},{"catalog_id":"3bd657af-017c-47c7-8642-5536567a1001","title":"Project Overview","description":"project-overview","extend":"{}","progress_status":"completed","repo_id":"fc565b46-fc33-4061-a286-40b2001ec17d","id":"8e1846cd-d55b-4111-86d7-17d896b9600c","gmt_create":"2026-01-28T00:23:52.0079334+03:00","gmt_modified":"2026-01-28T00:23:52.0110591+03:00"},{"catalog_id":"9d6b3554-62ce-4a9e-ba78-ededed4de99e","title":"Architecture Decision Records","description":"architecture-decision-records","extend":"{}","progress_status":"completed","repo_id":"fc565b46-fc33-4061-a286-40b2001ec17d","id":"652730c8-fa75-434c-b8f9-54b1f7258b96","gmt_create":"2026-01-28T00:23:58.3086431+03:00","gmt_modified":"2026-01-28T00:23:58.311736+03:00"},{"catalog_id":"4c6a73cb-175e-493e-b48c-4773e245bdd0","title":"Development Roadmap","description":"development-roadmap","extend":"{}","progress_status":"completed","repo_id":"fc565b46-fc33-4061-a286-40b2001ec17d","id":"a1d179ef-3ee3-4e04-98cf-8c5938e7edee","gmt_create":"2026-01-28T00:24:54.6604656+03:00","gmt_modified":"2026-01-28T00:24:54.6636671+03:00"},{"catalog_id":"bdf68ab6-3405-4f87-a795-6c7609a90a4c","title":"Technical Design Specifications","description":"technical-design-specifications","extend":"{}","progress_status":"completed","repo_id":"fc565b46-fc33-4061-a286-40b2001ec17d","id":"f55a6922-e110-41a8-bda0-61180692fed8","gmt_create":"2026-01-28T00:25:16.8063644+03:00","gmt_modified":"2026-01-28T00:25:16.8105531+03:00"}],"wiki_overview":{"content":"Based on my comprehensive analysis of the Timeskein project documentation, I can now generate a detailed project overview.\n\n\u003cblog\u003e\n\n# Timeskein: Personal Contextual Journal - Comprehensive Project Analysis\n\n## Project Introduction\n\nTimeskein is a **personal contextual journal system** designed to collect digital activity traces and transform them into structured episodes and threads of meaning. The project addresses a fundamental human challenge: remembering what we were working on, where we left off, and what needs attention next.\n\n### Purpose Statement\nTimeskein serves as a \"motif of threads\" that helps users untangle the complexity of digital work by collecting minimal context during work sessions and allowing them to quickly answer critical questions about their current workload and past activities.\n\n### Core Goals and Objectives\n- **Local-first processing**: Preprocessing and storage occurs locally by default, with optional server support\n- **Minimal data footprint**: Store only what's necessary for functionality (\"videorecorder of life\" is the anti-goal)\n- **Managed sensitivity**: Data marked by sensitivity levels (normal/private/sensitive) with policies governing storage, synchronization, and display\n- **Provenance tracking**: Every saved observation knows its source, timestamp, and applied rules\n\n### Target Audience\n- Knowledge workers managing multiple concurrent tasks and contexts\n- Professionals who rely on tickets, documents, chats, and repositories\n- Users seeking to maintain control over their digital work records\n\n## Technical Architecture\n\nThe Timeskein architecture follows a **multi-device, local-first design** with clear separation of concerns across components:\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    subgraph \"User Devices\"\n        Desktop[Desktop Surface]\n        Mobile[Android Surface]\n        Agent[Device Agent]\n    end\n    \n    subgraph \"System Components\"\n        Collectors[Collectors Pack]\n        Schema[TS-SCHEMA]\n        Sync[TS-SYNC]\n        Hub[TS-HUB]\n        Storage[Local Storage]\n    end\n    \n    Desktop --\u003e|\"Local API\"| Agent\n    Mobile --\u003e|\"Local API\"| Agent\n    Collectors --\u003e|\"Event Ingest API\"| Agent\n    Agent --\u003e Storage\n    Agent --\u003e Sync --\u003e Hub\n    Schema -.-\u003e Desktop\n    Schema -.-\u003e Mobile\n    Schema -.-\u003e Agent\n    Schema -.-\u003e Sync\n    Schema -.-\u003e Hub\n```\n\n### Component Breakdown\n\n**Device Agent (TS-AGENT)**: The central backend that maintains data integrity and executes all use-cases. It acts as the single point of truth on each device.\n\n**Surfaces (UI)**: Thin user interfaces that call into the local agent. Includes:\n- Desktop Surface (Windows/macOS) with command palette and tray\n- Android Surface with embedded agent\n- Future mobile app UI\n\n**Collectors**: Platform-specific activity collectors that gather signals and send normalized events to the agent.\n\n**Hub Backend (TS-HUB)**: Central backend for multi-device synchronization and data aggregation.\n\n**Schema Library (TS-SCHEMA)**: Shared contract library defining DTOs, versioning, and serialization.\n\n### Design Patterns\n\nThe system employs several key architectural patterns:\n\n- **Ports \u0026 Adapters Pattern**: Core domain logic isolated from infrastructure concerns\n- **Local-first Architecture**: All operations work offline, with optional synchronization\n- **Event Sourcing**: Append-only logs for context events and work item changes\n- **Hexagonal Architecture**: Clean separation between business logic and external systems\n\n## Key Implementation\n\n### Main Entry Points\n- **TS-AGENT**: Core device backend handling all business logic and data operations\n- **TS-DESKTOP**: Desktop UI host with command palette and tray integration\n- **TS-ANDROID**: Android application with embedded agent and surface\n- **TS-HUB**: Central backend for multi-device synchronization\n\n### Core Modules\n- **Work Item Management**: CRUD operations for work items with state tracking\n- **Ref Extraction Engine**: Normalized reference extraction from URLs and window titles\n- **Context Event Logging**: Append-only event logging with provenance tracking\n- **Inventory Calculation**: Deterministic computation of current work inventory\n\n### Configuration Approach\n- **Versioned Contracts**: Shared schema library ensures compatibility across components\n- **Denylist Policies**: Configurable application and domain blocking\n- **Privacy Controls**: Managed sensitivity levels and data minimization\n- **Local API**: Secure IPC/RPC interface between surfaces and agents\n\n### External Dependencies\n- **SQLite**: Primary storage with indexing for performance\n- **Web Container**: Shared UI across platforms (Electron/Tauri)\n- **Native Adapters**: Platform-specific integrations for OS capabilities\n- **Schema Library**: Versioned contracts for all inter-component communication\n\n### Integration Points\n- **Local API**: Surface-to-Agent communication via secure IPC\n- **Event Ingest API**: Collector-to-Agent event delivery\n- **Sync Protocol**: Multi-device replication via TS-HUB\n- **Connector Framework**: Optional integrations with external applications\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    participant User as User\n    participant UI as Desktop Surface\n    participant Agent as TS-AGENT\n    participant DB as Local Storage\n    \n    User-\u003e\u003eUI: Hotkey opens palette\n    User-\u003e\u003eUI: Select action (create/state/note/ref)\n    UI-\u003e\u003eAgent: Local API command\n    Agent-\u003e\u003eAgent: Validate \u0026 normalize\n    Agent-\u003e\u003eDB: Write/update\n    Agent--\u003e\u003eUI: Updated view\n    UI--\u003e\u003eUser: Display results\n```\n\n## Key Features\n\n### Functionality Overview\n\n**Manual-first Inventory**: The MVP focuses on providing a reliable way to track current work items through explicit user actions rather than automatic observation.\n\n**Work Item Lifecycle**: Complete management of work items including creation, state changes, note-taking, and reference attachment.\n\n**Contextual Awareness**: Minimal context collection through active window monitoring and browser integration, with strong privacy controls.\n\n**Multi-device Synchronization**: Optional synchronization across Windows, macOS, and Android devices.\n\n### Implementation Highlights\n\n**Inventory Management** (`docs/mvp/01_user_story_inventory.md`):\n- Real-time inventory listing with state indicators\n- Quick state changes via keyboard shortcuts\n- Reference management for external contexts\n- Pinning mechanism for priority items\n\n**Privacy-first Design** (`docs/adr/0001-initial-architecture.md`):\n- Denylist for applications and domains\n- Privacy-sensitive data handling\n- Local-only operation by default\n- Minimal data retention policies\n\n**Extensible Architecture** (`docs/rfc/0002-system-topology-and-component-map.md`):\n- Modular component design\n- Plugin-friendly collector system\n- Versioned API contracts\n- Future-proof evolution paths\n\n### Feature Architecture\n\n```mermaid\nstateDiagram-v2\n    [*] --\u003e Idle\n    Idle --\u003e Creating: Create Work Item\n    Idle --\u003e Managing: Manage Inventory\n    Creating --\u003e Idle: Saved\n    Managing --\u003e StateChange: Change State\n    Managing --\u003e RefAttach: Attach Reference\n    Managing --\u003e Touch: Touch Item\n    StateChange --\u003e Idle: Updated\n    RefAttach --\u003e Idle: Linked\n    Touch --\u003e Idle: Updated\n    Idle --\u003e [*]: Exit\n```\n\nThe system emphasizes **explicit user control** over data collection while maintaining the flexibility to evolve toward more automated features in future phases. The architecture ensures that privacy and local-first principles remain core tenets throughout the system's evolution.\n\n**Sources:**\n- [Project Overview](docs/00_project_overview.md)\n- [Initial Architecture Decision](docs/adr/0001-initial-architecture.md)\n- [MVP User Story](docs/mvp/01_user_story_inventory.md)\n- [System Topology](docs/rfc/0002-system-topology-and-component-map.md)\n- [Client Architecture](docs/rfc/0003-client-app-suite-architecture.md)\n- [MVP Design](docs/rfc/0001-mvp-inventory-design.md)\n- [Roadmap](docs/roadmap/0001-mvp-execution-roadmap.md)\n- [Manual Inventory UX](docs/mvp/02_manual_inventory_ui_ux.md)\n\n\u003c/blog\u003e","gmt_create":"2026-01-28T00:21:11.272433+03:00","gmt_modified":"2026-01-28T00:21:11.272433+03:00","id":"382587ab-dcc8-4a8e-a4bb-b91d93917ff2","repo_id":"fc565b46-fc33-4061-a286-40b2001ec17d"},"wiki_readme":{"content":"No readme file","gmt_create":"2026-01-28T00:18:45.7731618+03:00","gmt_modified":"2026-01-28T00:18:45.7731618+03:00","id":"d6ce7fd2-d66f-45aa-a5c4-9e65498d4409","repo_id":"fc565b46-fc33-4061-a286-40b2001ec17d"},"wiki_repo":{"id":"fc565b46-fc33-4061-a286-40b2001ec17d","name":"timeskein","progress_status":"completed","wiki_present_status":"COMPLETED","optimized_catalog":"\".\\n└── docs\\\\\\n    ├── adr\\\\\\n    │   └── 0001-initial-architecture.md\\n    ├── mvp\\\\\\n    │   ├── 01_user_story_inventory.md\\n    │   ├── 02_manual_inventory_ui_ux.md\\n    │   └── 02_user_story_manual_inventory.md\\n    ├── rfc\\\\\\n    │   ├── 0001-mvp-inventory-design.md\\n    │   ├── 0002-system-topology-and-component-map.md\\n    │   └── 0003-client-app-suite-architecture.md\\n    ├── roadmap\\\\\\n    │   └── 0001-mvp-execution-roadmap.md\\n    └── 00_project_overview.md\\n\"","current_document_structure":"WikiEncrypted:","catalogue_think_content":"WikiEncrypted:","recovery_checkpoint":"wiki_generation_completed","last_commit_id":"3b6fa7e829